    "created_at": "2020-05-02T01:02:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-05-02T01:02:50Z",
    "body": "The `HTMLMediaElement.volume` property, IMO, is a convenience to allow pages to provide a custom volume control UI. As such, UAs are free to interpret the volume property the same way they would any other volume slider provided by the platform, and the behavior of that page-provided volume slider will therefore match other volume UI provided elsewhere on the same platform, including as @LeoWattenberg said by limiting the maximum effective volume (represented by `volume = 1.0`), or as @domenic says by ignoring the volume property entirely!\r\n\r\nIf pages need precise, defined control over media element output (e.g., to match perceived volumes of two separate media elements), they can use the Web Audio API and the [GainNode](https://webaudio.github.io/web-audio-api/#gainnode), which is defined as linear.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
