    "created_at": "2026-08-15T19:45:15Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-08-15T19:45:15Z",
    "body": "It's interesting that this whole discussion took place under a misapprehension.\n\n@LeoWattenberg wrote in 2020, \"Even though it's not specified, browsers currently seem to use linear scaling, presumably because it's easier to build.\"\n\nAll the discussion that follows assumes the case that browsers uniformly use linear scaling.\n\nHowever, at the time that discussion took place, Firefox did not use linear scaling, at least on Windows. It used a Tapered volume control via the WASAPI audio API: [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/coreaudio/audio-tapered-volume-controls](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/coreaudio/audio-tapered-volume-controls).\n\nThat actually changed in May of 2021, while the debate on this page was taking place, and nobody noticed, including the Firefox developer who changed it from tapered to linear by accident. [https://github.com/mozilla/cubeb/commit/5ebe69cb3a1c7b022af85c04187456948b209d4b](https://github.com/mozilla/cubeb/commit/5ebe69cb3a1c7b022af85c04187456948b209d4b)\n\nThat's a very important point -- for most people using web browsers, the difference is not noticeable.  To see the effect, you have to listening with hardware that can produce a wide dynamic range, and controlling the volume of what you are listening to via the browser.  Most often, if you are connecting a laptop or desktop to an amplifier, you are controlling volume in the OS or in the sound hardware directly.  If you are using a mobile device, you are probably controlling volume in the device not the web page.\n\nHowever, for the small number of users who can tell the difference, the tapered volume control is always superior.  The explanations for this are already linked, including the Microsoft page I linked in this comment.\n\nThere is always the other option of controlling the volume in the OS instead, and I suspect, admittedly without evidence, that people gravitate towards doing that because the OS volume control works properly and controlling volume in the browser does not.\n\nThere is a suggestion above that some web pages might be correcting to a tapered volume, and are therefore relying on the assumption of linearity.  Again, until a few years ago that assumption was false in the case of Firefox, so it is unlikely. I thought I had found one in [Jellyfin](https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin-web/blob/bb9868218c872b7c115466c9ff1ef4aec4af7d0d/src/plugins/htmlAudioPlayer/plugin.js#L530), but on closer inspection it is using the GainNode that @jernoble mentioned.\n\nIncidentally, the reason why browsers are in fact inconsistent is that it is not true, as Leo suggested initially, that linear scaling is easier.  It depends whether the browser is making volume adjustments to the audio stream itself, or passing them on to the OS to apply.  OS audio handling, at least in Windows and Linux that I am aware of, uses tapered volume adjustment.  The accidental change from tapered to linear in 2021 was a side-effect of switching from applying the volume adjustment via WASAPI to applying it directly.  I would guess that was the motivation for leaving the volume handling undefined in the standard originally: it was assumed that it would be left to the OS and was not therefore easily controlled by the browser software.\n\nI've been patching Firefox on Linux for my own use since 2022 to correct this bug. A recent change in Firefox from using Pulseaudio for volume control to applying volume directly required me to write a new patch, which I will submit shortly.  Incidentally, I am using the cubic function to approximate the exponential curve; that matches what PulseAudio does, and also matches what Jellyfin is doing via GainNode.  The Dr Lex page that Leo linked also suggests that, though he prefers the quartic.  It does not matter much -- the traditional \"tapered potentiometer\" used in analog volume control circuits is described as \"logarithmic\" but is frequently implemented as two linear sections.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
