    "body": "> However, at the time that discussion took place, Firefox did not use linear scaling, at least on Windows. It used a Tapered volume control via the WASAPI audio API [...] That actually changed in May of 2021, while the debate on this page was taking place, and nobody noticed, including the Firefox developer who changed it from tapered to linear by accident.\n\nThis is not correct. Firefox on Windows did not use the audio-tapered endpoint volume control for `HTMLMediaElement.volume`. It used `IAudioStreamVolume`. The Microsoft page you linked clearly distinguishes the two: `IAudioEndpointVolume` is the audio-tapered endpoint/device API, while `IAudioStreamVolume` is explicitly listed among the interfaces using a linear-tapered curve.\n\nmozilla/cubeb@5ebe69c was not an accidental change from tapered to linear either. I wrote that change deliberately. We were investigating reports of Firefox being quieter than Chrome on some hardware/driver combinations when .volume was reduced. The bug at the time explicitly says that we expected `IAudioStreamVolume` to use a linear taper, while Chrome scaled the samples internally. The change made cubeb do the same.\n\nThe claim that Firefox historically provided a perceptual/tapered mapping for `.volume`, and accidentally lost it in 2021, is not correct.",
